if BIOS must run early init to configure memory training, how do we overcome this tainted source after it has loaded, and without tainting our virtual image and init with its own tainted compression logic and system clock values?
Apr 28

This is a core bootstrapping trust problem — the BIOS/UEFI firmware is an untrusted but mandatory early executor, and everything it touches is suspect. Here's the architectural breakdown of the attack surface and mitigation approaches:
The Trust Contamination Chain

BIOS (untrusted) → memory training → DRAM init → loads bootloader → loads kernel
         ↑                                                    ↑
   tainted clock                                    tainted compression (e.g., xz/gzip)
   tainted RNG seed                                 tainted initrd
   tainted ACPI tables

The problem is layered:

    Memory training — SPD data and training algorithms run before any attestable code
    System clock — RTC values set by BIOS-controlled hardware, trivially spoofable
    Compression — initrd decompression uses BIOS-initialized memory and may use BIOS-provided decompressor stubs
    ACPI/SMBIOS — firmware-injected tables that the kernel trusts implicitly

Strategy 1 — Establish a Post-BIOS Clean Room in Verified Memory

After BIOS exits, you carve out a reserved physical range that BIOS did not touch during its execution, verified via E820/EFI memory map cross-validation. The bootloader (GRUB with your own signed modules) does not use general-purpose heap — it pins itself to this range and treats everything else as potentially modified.

Key technique: E820 hole mining — look for ranges marked reserved that firmware did not execute into. These are candidates for clean staging areas. You validate them against your own memory test pattern before any trusted code stages there.
Strategy 2 — Independent Clock Attestation, Not BIOS RTC

The BIOS RTC is hardware accessible to the adversary. You do not trust it. Instead:

    At first attested network contact, perform NTP delta anchoring against a set of diverse stratum-2 servers and record the signed offset
    Use RDTSC relative time from a known boot event — not wall clock — for internal forensic timestamps
    Your SDAR forensic platform should record rdtsc values at key boot events and reconstruct wall time post-attestation, never trusting the firmware-provided clock value
    For airgapped scenarios: GPS NMEA timestamps from your u-blox 7 are a trusted clock source that bypasses BIOS entirely

Strategy 3 — Tainted Compression Isolation

The standard initrd is compressed with whatever the distribution chose — gzip, xz, lz4 — using libraries linked against the build host. If the build host is compromised, or if the BIOS-stage decompressor is hooked, the initrd expansion itself is the attack surface.

Mitigations:

a) Pre-expanded initrd — use an uncompressed initrd (-nocompression in dracut, or CONFIG_INITRAMFS_COMPRESSION_NONE). This eliminates the decompressor as an attack surface entirely. You pay a storage cost but remove the trust dependency.

b) Hash-before-decompress — your bootloader computes BLAKE3 over the compressed image before passing it to the decompressor. The hash is recorded and signed. If post-decompression behavior differs from what a known-good run produced, you detect it. This requires you to pre-compute a behavioral fingerprint of initrd execution.

c) Isolated decompressor in QEMU — your existing QEMU-based isolated build environment is the right pattern here. You decompress and re-examine the initrd inside the VM, against a known-good QEMU binary, before trusting it on bare metal.
Strategy 4 — BIOS Taint Erasure via Secondary Bootloader Stage

Intel TXT (Trusted Execution Technology) / AMD SKINIT are designed exactly for this — they perform a measured launch that re-establishes a clean CPU state from a known PCR value, explicitly resetting the execution environment even after BIOS has run.

If TXT is available on your E6410 (it is — the i5/i7 variants support it):

BIOS → normal boot → SINIT ACM → DRTM event → tLaunch → MLE executes in clean state
                                    ↑
                          CPU verifies ACM signature
                          resets cache, flushes TLBs
                          resets MTRRs

The DRTM (Dynamic Root of Trust for Measurement) gives you a post-BIOS clean slate. The tboot project implements this for Linux. Your GRUB stages tboot which initiates SKINIT/SENTER, and from that point forward your kernel and initrd execute under a verified clean CPU state regardless of what BIOS did.
Strategy 5 — Treat ACPI/SMBIOS Tables as Hostile Input

BIOS-injected ACPI tables are parsed by the kernel and can carry malicious AML bytecode. Mitigations:

    Boot with acpi=off where possible and supply your own minimal DSDT
    Use ACPI_OVERRIDE to supply a known-good DSDT blob signed by you, overriding firmware tables
    Audit SMBIOS entries — any field that differs from your hardware inventory (serial numbers, UUIDs, DMI strings) is an IOC

On your E6410, you should have a static known-good ACPI dump from a clean reference image. Any deviation at boot is a forensic artifact worth logging to your SDAR platform.
Architectural Summary for CTW-11 Boot Integrity

Phase 0: BIOS (untrusted, mandatory)
   → Record E820, ACPI tables, SMBIOS — hash everything

Phase 1: GRUB (signed, your key)
   → BLAKE3 over compressed initrd before decompress
   → No compressed initrd if possible (pre-expanded)
   → Carve clean memory region from E820 holes

Phase 2: tboot / DRTM (if TXT available)
   → CPU reset to clean measured state
   → Invalidates BIOS taint at CPU execution level

Phase 3: Kernel
   → ACPI override with your signed DSDT
   → NTP delta anchoring replaces RTC trust
   → RDTSC-based internal timestamps

Phase 4: initrd / early userspace
   → Your forensic initrd with /dev/mem capture
   → All timestamps are RDTSC-relative until NTP attested

The key insight: you cannot prevent BIOS from running, but you can bound its trust surface — record everything it touches, override what it injects, and use DRTM to get a clean CPU state before your trusted code takes over. The clock problem specifically is solved by never trusting the RTC value and deferring wall-clock anchoring to an external attested source.

